cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I agree with 1@D by West although some would bid 1@H. First of all, if East has hearts they can bid it. Also, since West has a strong hand they can bid hearts later. If West had a weak hand with hearts and diamonds, then 1@H might be better to get the suit in before the opponents interfere in spades. But with a strong hand West can still get the heart suit in later.|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BWA Kib: ^*nAren't we always going for a major fit first?|
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Yes. You optimize the bidding to find a major suit fit. But since West is so strong, you are never going to miss your fit if you have it and here it is possible they have a diamond slam since West has 17 starting points.|
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The hand has 15 HCP, 2 quality suit, plus 3.5 quick tricks which is above average. So that is why 1@D is a better bid than 1@H IMO as it is much more flexible and has no real disadvantage.|
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


mb|P|mb|1S|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|mb|3N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I like the 3N call as well since East did not jump shift, as they did last hand. East is limited. Therefore, slam is very remote and 3N will be the best spot the vast majority of the time.|
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


