cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In general yes. But having said that, although I still may open 1@C if my diamonds are strong and clubs are weak, I will open 1@D. |
PG| ***** 112 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Here, your diamonds are very strong, but your clubs are not that weak with 2 honors. So it is a tossup between 1@D and 1@C.|
PG| ***** 113 ***** |


mb|1N|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|2D|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West should double 2@D as lead directing. Lead directing doubles are very useful for the defense. It is often hard for partner to find the best opening lead. So anything you can do to help partner is good.|
PG| ***** 114 ***** |


mb|D|mb|2H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Once West doubles, note the following about what North can bid. Had West passed, North's only choice is to bid 2@H (or rarely super accept when holding 4 card support). But once West doubles, North has 2 more bids available. They are pass and redouble.|
PG| ***** 115 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Redouble shows a doubleton honor in hearts (usually the Ace or King). So the 2@H bid here promises 3 card support.|
